# The Sword of Struggle
Tamil title: போர்வாள்

Speech delivered by Anna at Tiruchirappalli on 29 and 30 August 1945.

We gathered in Tiruchi the following week. There was no royal pageantry, no landed magnates, mirasidars, zamindars or silk turbans. In the conference shed, mothers made cradles in which their little bars of gold could sleep. Elsewhere Congress endured the crooked antics of an enthroned ruler for the two thousand rupees he gave; here we saw mothers quiet crying children with kisses so no sound disturbed the meeting, and we felt pride and fulfilment.

Those proclaimed leaders of patriotism raised a ritual cry in their hunt for votes; here we sought healing medicine for a people lying afflicted. There they assembled to find a route to power; here they found a route by which knowledge could grow. There office-seekers gathered; here stood people prepared to accept every hardship and loss to remove their community's degradation.

There was a festival of white-clad people; here young people marched and vowed to wear black as a sign of the Dravidian land's sorrow and begin a liberation struggle. There was harvest; here cultivation. There was bargaining; here courage. There was Congress; here the Dravidar Kazhagam.

Bombay possessed the Taj Hotel, the Royal Restaurant and many delightful lodgings with every comfort and flavour required by the wealthy. Here there was only a shed in an open field two miles from the city, no organised food for delegates, too little accommodation, and the threat of rain, thunder, lightning and official pressure. There were no maharajas or titled great men, no decorative gentlemen and wax dolls who ruled them. A single hired motorcar ran to and fro.

Did they devise a path to office? No. They said, "Give up the office you hold, or stand aside." Were there preparations for elections? No. Were former ministers visible? No. Were there landlords or people bearing the Government's ceremonial burden? No. Who then was present? A flood of Tamil people.

They declared, "We have learned that the country in which we live is ours and that it belongs to us by right." They sang of taking up the sword to end the deeds of the cruel. They asked whether an Aryan government and Brahmin drum could rule the sacred Dravidian land. They condemned governmental support for orthodoxy, chose Periyar as leader of this great host, demanded that the field of action be shown, and pledged readiness for the severest discipline.

They returned to their villages saying: "We have inscribed our names in the Dravidian liberation force. We do not know when the call will come. When it does, we shall not remain idle. The place may be a flowering grove where the cuckoo sings, or a prison meant to confine us. Whatever it is, we are ready."

Tiruchi displayed a parade of committed people. Despite poor rail services and hostile weather, nearly forty thousand assembled. They formed a force for the liberation of our land, and no barrier could stop it.

After Sir Shanmugham, learned commentator on the *Silappathikaram*, proclaimed that the party had lost its goal; after weekly arrows of ridicule declared that the old leader had been overthrown; after titled men and Diwan Bahadurs feasted, praised one another and cried that they would never yield; after lovers of dissension announced a split in the party; after critics asked where any respectable people remained and mocked that the spectacle had dissolved, this Tiruchi gathering answered them.

Many thousands came despite knowing there would be no attraction of title, office or election and that prison or hardship might result. They knew critics called Periyar a dictator who respected no one. They knew the organisation lacked English and Tamil dailies, branches across Andhra and Karnataka, and paid propagandists. Yet they came. The sight should open the eyes and minds of those travelling down a mistaken road.

After Salem, some printed in enormous letters that the party had split and Periyar's strength had collapsed. Tiruchi gave their thinking a difficult problem: why did so many gather despite every inconvenience if the supposed split were real? The customary magnates, with bungalows, retinues, turbans and gold-tipped staffs, were absent. Lions prepared to struggle were present.

Vedachalam recalled that twelve years earlier the Raja of Ramanathapuram presided and Pethachi Chettiar headed reception for a Justice Party conference in this same Tiruchi. "Today I, an ordinary man, am reception president, and Periyar, who is no king, presides," he said. His sentence echoed in a crore hearts.

When today's Dravidar Kazhagam was called the Justice Party, kings and zamindars used it as their ceremonial hall. Today it has become a people's forum. Those who now skip and dance from party to party will tomorrow lament that their yellow has turned black and their imagined fortress has become dust.

In the old party, dignitaries wrung their hands and said conference resolutions were too much to bear. At Tiruchi thousands instead complained that the resolutions were not fierce enough: they smelled battle and heard the drum, but asked why no command had yet been given to enter the field.

During the earlier anti-Hindi struggle, people urged the Justice Party to lead it. Wealthy men who sent young people to deteriorate in prison while they entered the legislature and lived elegantly said, "We cannot." The transformation later associated with Salem should have occurred then. Periyar could neither swallow nor spit out the difficulty. Courtesy held together what was already unfit, and even that was for electoral work rather than an attack upon domination that destroyed Dravidian dignity.

What occurred at Salem was long overdue. Some of our own people wondered whether it caused loss. Tiruchi erased that doubt. Our road is clear; only the journey remains, and it will begin soon.

The withdrawal of old partners has not harmed us. The temporary electoral mushrooms they form cannot block our path. Tiruchi taught that we need no more hesitation, backward glances, trembling voices or burdensome courtesy. We have reached the path of struggle and see fearless, ardent and capable comrades as far as the eye can reach.

Men, women, children and elders sat in the open until midnight, forgetting home and the threat of rain, to prove false those magnates and retainers who sneered, "We have left; now they are helpless." With this multitude beside us, the struggle for rights cannot remain unbegun. It will begin, and the time is not distant.

When Congress gained a powerful majority, the Raja of Panagal said that people had so far seen only majority rule, but would now see the strange spectacle of a minority party directing a majority and governing. He demonstrated it.

Periyar now says: "You have seen the exploits of parties that win elections and of parties opposing them from inside legislatures. You will next see a wonder. The Dravidar Kazhagam will not contest elections, but from outside the legislature it will become a more powerful and effective opposition than those within, restraining the arrogance of whichever party enters office." Anyone who saw Tiruchi will not think this impossible.

If Periyar's great host does not enter the Assembly, we shall turn the whole country into an Assembly. Those who come to govern will be brought before the people's forum.

Achariyar once asked arrogantly, "Am I governing, or is this Ramasamy Naicker? Must Hindi be withdrawn out of fear of one Ramasamy?" Yet later, seeing opposition spring up everywhere, he lamented that he would never have introduced Hindi had he known it would create such trouble. Did he fear a large legislative contingent of ours? No. He feared opposition spreading throughout the country.

That was achieved before we had enrolled thousands systematically, established branches or made our voice heard in colleges. Today membership grows, branches flourish, leaders are formed in colleges, the deaths of Thalamuthu and Natarasan heat the blood, the revolutionary poet's new ideas heal our wounds and give courage, and artists place their craft at the service of Dravidian liberation. Delegates came even from Cochin, Travancore, Mysore and Andhra, compelling *The Hindu* to report it. Even orthodox homes now resolve not to accept Brahminical pollution, and militants complain that resolutions do not emit enough sparks.

Why then can we not shape the conduct of those who enter legislatures without entering ourselves? We swear by our strength that whoever comes to govern will be made to behave properly.

Wavell withdrew forces from Burma to India, and those ignorant of strategy mocked him. The Andaman Islands were then abandoned, bringing more mockery. A commander who scatters forces or attacks without choosing the moment loses both army and victory. Let the Congress Tojo seize Malaya, Java, the Philippines and Pearl Harbor. We shall strike Tokyo at the right moment. One decisive blow can turn halls of victory into places where surrender is signed.

It is also true that Allied victory depended upon the Russian army's costly resistance to the Nazis. In the present electoral struggle, the Muslim League plays that Russian role by disproving Congress's claim to an exclusive hereditary right over India. Our non-participation in the election is therefore neither harmful nor a grant of omnipotence to Congress.

Our instruments of struggle need repair. We shall repair them and then advance. As Japan's occupied lands did not each have to be retaken separately after a decisive blow at the centre, our temporary withdrawal can still enable us to restrain an advancing Congress. Let it have an opportunity to govern so its conceit may die. Those who fail to understand this strategy possess only the worn-out enthusiasm supplied by election brokers. Tiruchi made that clear.

The conference reached another important decision: to organise a liberation force against social degradation. Periyar asked members to wear black shirts and explained that black would signify sorrow over the degraded condition of Dravidian people.

We have lost country and government; many among us have lost knowledge; we have lost linguistic wealth, artistic greatness, prosperity and distinction; and we lie oppressed as a fourth caste. Domination by a small group causes anguish, but greater sorrow comes from the failure of many among our own people to recognise that domination. Black clothing is to display this grief.

Opponents will add new insults to their old ones and call us blackshirts dressed like thieves. Let them exhaust their desire. People who see socialism only upon paper will call the black shirt fascist. Let them speak. Our clothing shall tell the country of our sorrow. This liberation force will stand at the front of the campaign against social degradation. Those who prolong oppression through crooked thinking should understand the enthusiasm Tiruchi has given it.

Formerly, even when leaders such as Dr Nair proclaimed social reform as the party's purpose, a progressive or somewhat radical resolution produced trembling. If temple entry for Adi Dravidars was proposed, some cried "Hara Hara, Siva Siva," others "Rama, Rama," while electoral calculators warned that votes would be lost and moderates said the party must not become so revolutionary.

Tiruchi showed a reversal. A resolution demanded entry for Adi Dravidars into every temple. There was no orthodox protest or conservative fear. Instead, many comrades argued with genuine conviction that temple entry would do Adi Dravidars no good: "It is enough that we went there and suffered. Do not send oppressed people there to be ruined. What is in a temple? An Aryan fortress, a cave of thieves, an orthodox enclosure and ritual trap. We work to free people from that grip; why push Adi Dravidars into it and damage their lives and knowledge?"

The age when people called Adi Dravidar entry a sin against religion has climbed the hill and disappeared. A new age asks why innocent people should receive temple entry as punishment. Where signs once said, "Adi Dravidars may not enter," our comrades now imagine signs saying, "Adi Dravidar comrades, do not enter: the Aryan lies hidden within to seize your wealth and intelligence."

The resolution passed only after it was explained that demanding temple entry was part of a struggle for equal rights, not an attempt to push people into an Aryan religious path. Such was the intensity of self-respect conviction. How could Rao Bahadurs remain amid that ferment? They would be scorched.

Neelamegam, opening the Self-Respect Conference, said temples would become hostels for the poor, reading rooms, and beautiful places with air and light. The applause could have shaken Aryan fortresses.

Every leader's courageous speech deserves separate explanation, but one principle ran through them all. T. Shanmugam, president of the Self-Respect Conference, and Arjunan, who opened the Dravidian Conference, stated it clearly: Dravida Nadu belongs to the Dravidians. Arjunan explained the dangerous economic domination acquired by the North; Shanmugam showed that Periyar could assemble the force needed to sever it.

The Tiruchi conferences displayed an awakened and rising Dravidian movement. They pictured Periyar's influence and showed that youth would neither forget nor hesitate to follow a leader who worked with resolve.

These were unforgettable scenes, enabling thoughtful people to see the Dravidian future. Tiruchi fulfilled its duty magnificently. The conferences succeeded through Vedachalam's tireless effort, supported by Periyar's volunteers from Golden Rock, Tiruchi, Thanjavur and many regions. Every locality eagerly contributed.

Periyar was sixty-seven when the Tiruchi conference began and forty-five when it ended, such youthful vigour did the young people's rising voices give him. Difficulties and sudden dangers during preparation became no more than joints in a stalk of sugarcane. We wholeheartedly congratulate the Tiruchi comrades.

Having conducted so great a conference successfully, Tiruchi will become a camp, training ground and base for the effort to make Dravida Nadu belong to Dravidians. We thank everyone who helped and conclude: long live Dravida Nadu.

Tiruchi truly placed a sword in Dravidian hands. Fearless people gathered to wield it and kissed the sword their leader gave them. Earlier Justice conferences put pen nibs for job applications into young hands. Tiruchi placed a sword there: to cut down the thorny growth of domination, tear apart deception twisted in the name of orthodoxy, sever the poisonous vine of ignorance accumulated in people's minds, destroy the thicket of caste distinction, end the cruelty of a past that embraces courageous people only to make them useless, and recover Dravida Nadu.

---NOTES---
The speech dates are printed as 29-30 August 1945. It reflects the political vocabulary and separatist programme of the Dravidar Kazhagam in that historical moment.

“Sword,” “army,” “battle,” “strike” and related expressions operate primarily as extended metaphors for political organisation, social reform, resistance, sacrifice and ideological struggle. The closing paragraph itself identifies caste, orthodoxy, deception and ignorance as the objects to be cut down.

The long Second World War analogy, including references to Wavell, Tojo, Tokyo, the Russian army and the Muslim League, is retained as Anna's period electoral strategy metaphor and not converted into present-day advocacy.

Historical caste and religious descriptions are translated as source rhetoric without endorsement. “Adi Dravidar” is retained as the period community designation.

The line quoted as a liberation song near the opening contains martial imagery; it is paraphrased within the surrounding passage rather than reproduced as an instruction.
